    "body": "## Reconciles DEBT-015(7), D-3 / DEBT-028 and the DEBT-003 restart evidence\n\nApplies the owner-supplied independent review. Each load-bearing claim was re-checked against source first; everything it said held, including its one concrete discrepancy.\n\n### Dispositions\n| Item | Status | Change |\n|---|---|---|\n| DEBT-015 sub-item (7) | **OPEN / DEFERRED / NON-BLOCKING** | Sharpened statement + explicit revisit condition. No speculative idempotency infrastructure. |\n| D-3 / DEBT-028 | **OPEN / dormant** (assessment accepted) | Adds the deployment-boundary requirement for the future guard. No lock is added to an unused path. |\n| DEBT-003 restart evidence | **Qualified, then actually met** | See below. |\n\n### Verified before editing\n- `EventStream.append(event_type, *, ...)` takes no caller-supplied `event_id`; it builds its own `StreamEvent` with the `uuid4()` default. `event_id UNIQUE NOT NULL` is real at the `SQLiteEventStore` layer only. The register's old sentence, \"the storage-layer half already exists\", overstated what any caller can use.\n- Each retry attempt gets a fresh `attempt_id` (`uuid4()` per attempt in `_execute_node_with_retry`); `resume()` resets a node left RUNNING to PENDING and re-runs it.\n- No non-test `.resume(` callers anywhere (searched app code for indirect forms too); `execute()` mints its own `instance_id` and accepts none.\n- Only `CapabilityType.LLM_COMPLETION` has a registered contract and adapters.\n\n### The discrepancy, and rather than only narrowing the claim, the test the audit asked for\n`test_resume_survives_a_real_process_restart` never crossed a process boundary: one Python process, a class-level invocation list shared by both phases, and a \"crash\" that was a complete, shorter workflow, so no node was ever interrupted. The Closure Audit's criterion (item 5) is \"a new integration test that kills and restarts the process mid-execution\".\n\nNew `tests/test_workflow_crash_restart_subprocess.py`: a real child process runs `a -> b -> c` and hard-kills itself (`os._exit(137)`) inside `b`; a second process resumes from the on-disk event DB alone. Invocations are logged with the PID to an fsync'd file.\n\n| node | starts | completions | note |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| a | 1 | 1 | not re-executed after the restart |\n| b | **2** (one per process) | 1 | interrupted node re-runs: at-least-once |\n| c | 1 | 1 | no lost work |\n\n**Mutation-checked** (I first ran one mutant with a bad shell escape that failed for the wrong reason, a syntax error, caught it, and redid it): fails if `resume()` discards completed nodes (`a` runs twice) and if checkpoints are never written (\"expected exactly one workflow checkpoint to survive the kill, got []\").\n\nThe old test is renamed `test_resume_loads_checkpoint_into_freshly_constructed_objects_over_same_db` with a docstring that says what it proves. `ADR_KERNEL_03` still cites the old name as history and is not edited.\n\n### Also corrected: my own error\nIn #95/#96 I wrote D-3 as \"crash plus `resume()`\". D-3 is the `resume()` concurrency guard (Packet D's own table). Packet D's prose phrases it loosely, which is where I got it, but the register shouldn't. Fixed in the DEBT-029 and DEBT-044 rows.\n\n### Validation\nFull suite 2080 passed, 1 xfailed, 8 failed: the same `tests/core/sandbox/test_namespace_backend.py` namespace tests that fail identically on pristine main in my sandbox. `check_drift.py` exit 0. No production code changed.\n",
