  {
    "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/1h0lde4/ocbrain-v4.1/pulls/64",
    "id": 4756841197,
    "node_id": "PR_kwDOSWs39c8AAAABG4ei7Q",
    "html_url": "https://github.com/1h0lde4/ocbrain-v4.1/pull/64",
    "diff_url": "https://github.com/1h0lde4/ocbrain-v4.1/pull/64.diff",
    "patch_url": "https://github.com/1h0lde4/ocbrain-v4.1/pull/64.patch",
    "issue_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/1h0lde4/ocbrain-v4.1/issues/64",
    "number": 64,
    "state": "closed",
    "locked": false,
    "title": "test: D7 heartbeat tests - write the heartbeat atomically (fixes a truncate-then-read race)",
    "user": {
      "login": "1h0lde4",
      "id": 196927701,
      "node_id": "U_kgDOC7zg1Q",
      "avatar_url": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/196927701?v=4",
      "gravatar_id": "",
      "url": "https://api.github.com/users/1h0lde4",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/1h0lde4",
      "followers_url": "https://api.github.com/users/1h0lde4/followers",
      "following_url": "https://api.github.com/users/1h0lde4/following{/other_user}",
      "gists_url": "https://api.github.com/users/1h0lde4/gists{/gist_id}",
      "starred_url": "https://api.github.com/users/1h0lde4/starred{/owner}{/repo}",
      "subscriptions_url": "https://api.github.com/users/1h0lde4/subscriptions",
      "organizations_url": "https://api.github.com/users/1h0lde4/orgs",
      "repos_url": "https://api.github.com/users/1h0lde4/repos",
      "events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/1h0lde4/events{/privacy}",
      "received_events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/1h0lde4/received_events",
      "type": "User",
      "user_view_type": "public",
      "site_admin": false
    },
    "body": "## What\n\nFixes a **test defect** in the two D7 heartbeat tests of `tests/core/sandbox/test_docker_backend.py`. Test file only; `docker_backend.py` is untouched. This is outside B00 (the sync PR #60 is merged) and is not a known-failures entry.\n\n- **Cause:** the grandchild wrote its heartbeat with `date +%s%N > /workspace/heartbeat`. The redirect truncates the file before writing, so a host-side read can see an empty file. `test_d7_cancel_reaches_a_grandchild_that_was_provably_alive_before` then fires its own vacuity guard (\"the grandchild was not alive and advancing before cancel()\"; captured `before_2 == ''`).\n- **Fix:** one shared writer, `_HEARTBEAT_LOOP`, writes a temp file and renames it (`date > .heartbeat.tmp && mv -f .heartbeat.tmp heartbeat`), used by both heartbeat tests (the older `test_cancel_kills_full_process_tree` shared the writer). A reader now sees the previous value or the new one.\n- No assertion was weakened, no retry was added, nothing was added to any known-failures list.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- **Mechanism, measured:** the test's old writer gave 12,987 empty reads out of 1,621,719 (about 0.8% of the time); the new writer gave **0 empty and 0 missing reads out of 3,682,713** (25 s tight-loop host reads against a real container).\n- **Targeted runs:** the D7 provably-alive test **0 failures in 50**; the older full-process-tree test **0 in 50**.\n- **Mutation checks** (each restored byte-identical): (A) `cancel()` kills nothing and (B) `cancel()` sends SIGTERM, which the PID 1 shell ignores: **both tests fail** (detected). (C) the grandchild never writes: both fail on their vacuity guards. (D) the heartbeat is written once and then frozen: the D7 provably-alive test fails (detected); the **older test still passes**, which is the known limitation recorded in the D7 test's docstring (it compares two readings taken after cancel and cannot tell frozen from stopped). Unchanged by this PR.\n- mypy clean on the file; architecture drift check 15/15; diff is 13 insertions and 5 deletions in one file.\n\n## Full sandbox suite: not 0 failures, and why (disclosed)\n\n15 full `tests/core/sandbox` runs on this branch: **14 passed 147/147; 1 run had 1 failure, in a different test: `test_d11_run_plus_destroy_concurrently_no_hang_no_orphan`.** It is not a heartbeat test and this PR does not touch it. I did not hide it or call it environmental; I investigated it:\n\n- **Reproduced under load** (6 concurrent workers plus CPU burners on a 1-core VM): `assert COMPLETED == ERROR`, with `exit_code=137`.\n- **Independent of this PR:** the same failure reproduces on the untouched pre-fix `sandbox-fabric` tip `23fff0b` (3 of 36 runs under the same load).\n- **Mechanism (code read):** when `destroy()` kills and removes a container that `run()` is waiting on, `run()` inspects the exit code afterwards. If the container is already removed, the inspect yields nothing and `_classify` returns `ERROR`; if the inspect lands between the kill and the removal, it reads exit 137 and `_classify` returns `COMPLETED`, because nothing records that `destroy()` caused the kill. The outcome therefore depends on timing.\n- **Classification:** a **product inconsistency** in the `run()` + `destroy()` outcome (D11: \"asserting a consistent outcome each time\"), not a test defect. It also means the D11 PASS recorded in the reconciliation (§19.1) holds for unloaded runs only. The test's assertion matches the intended contract; it should not be weakened.\n- **Not fixed here.** A separate PR is proposed (record that destroy was requested, as `cancel_requested` already does, and classify accordingly), pending a ruling.\n\n## Merging\n\nMerge commit only. The user asked for the merge once the suite is clean; because of the D11 finding it is **held for an explicit decision**.\n",
