    "body": "## B1 — FZ-04: `AdaptiveSemaphore` permanent permit loss under concurrent holders\n\n**Scope:** `core/runtime/resilience.py` + one new test module. Nothing else. FZ-01/02/03/05/06/07 behavior and the ADR-KERNEL-07 D-4 strict xfail are untouched.\n\n### Defect (FZ-04)\n`AdaptiveSemaphore` kept `_acquired` / `_start_time` on the shared instance. With ≥2 concurrent holders the first `__aexit__` cleared the shared flag, and every later `__aexit__` returned early **without releasing its permit** — capacity was lost permanently. Latency was also measured from whichever holder wrote `_start_time` last.\n\n### Fix\n- One `_Hold` record per successful acquisition, kept in a per-task LIFO stack (`self._holds`). `__aexit__` pops its own hold; nothing about one holder lives on the shared instance.\n- **No lock added** to \"solve\" this: the release/adapt section has no `await`, so it is atomic on the loop; a test pins that property.\n- An exit with no matching hold is logged at error level and releases nothing (cross-task / unbalanced use).\n- Added read-only `snapshot()` and a keyword-only injectable `clock=` (default `time.perf_counter`) so latency-driven tests are deterministic.\n- Invariant enforced and tested: `available + held + handoff == current_limit + drain_count`, where `handoff` is a permit `Semaphore.release()` has already handed to a parked waiter that has not resumed yet. The two-term form `available + held == current_limit + drain_count` therefore holds whenever no hand-off is pending — in particular **idle ⇒ `available == current_limit + drain_count`**. A `snapshot()` read inside the hand-off window is exactly 1 low per pending hand-off; nothing is lost (see Review dispositions).\n- AIMD / EMA / drain logic itself is unchanged.\n\n### Evidence\n| | before | after |\n|---|---|---|\n| limit after growth to max | 10 | 10 |\n| idle `available` | 1 | 10 |\n| permits lost | 9 | 0 |\n| effective concurrency of a 10-wide burst | 1–2 | 10 |\n\n- **Tests:** 21 new in `tests/test_adaptive_semaphore_permit_conservation.py` — genuine concurrent holders, burst, resize, cancellation (waiting and holding), timeout (waiting and holding), held failure, 40 seeded repeated cycles, recovery, nested holds, per-holder latency, the hand-off window, production `safe_llm_call` path.\n- **Red/green:** the new tests fail on the pre-fix class and pass after; mutation checks are killed.\n- **Flakiness:** 40/40 repeated runs green.\n- **Full suite (system Python 3.12) on the final PR head tree, `main` (`69df55f`) merged in:** 1672 passed / 1 xfailed (the strict ADR-KERNEL-07 D-4 xfail, untouched). Earlier: `4ab5345` baseline 1651 / 1 xfailed.\n\n### Change-review notes (PROJECT_INSTRUCTIONS §18.3)\n- **Governance:** concurrency limiter only; no authority boundary added; no governor bypass.\n- **Replay/determinism:** no event schema change; `clock=` makes latency paths deterministic under test.\n- **Observability:** new error log on unmatched exit; `snapshot()` is read-only.\n- **Security:** none claimed; this is a capacity-conservation fix, not a sandbox/authz change.\n- **Compatibility:** constructor gains a keyword-only arg with the old default; public surface otherwise unchanged. Documented limitation: `__aenter__`/`__aexit__` must run in the same task.\n- **File note:** `resilience.py` is CRLF; line endings preserved (diff is the class only).\n- **Rollback:** revert this single commit.\n\n### Not in this PR\n`KNOWN_ISSUES.md` / FZ-04 closure bookkeeping is deferred to the Phase F reconciliation; this PR does not mark FZ-04 closed. Closure requires the required checks below to run green on the PR.\n\n### Review dispositions (Graphify advisory review, both items marked \"NOT verified\" by the bot)\n1. **\"Task-keyed holds leak permits when an async context exits in a different task\"** — **ACCEPTED, documented residual; not a defect on any production path.** It is the stated limitation in the class docstring, pinned by `test_exit_without_matching_hold_is_reported_and_releases_nothing`. The only production caller, `core/runtime/limits.py::safe_llm_call`, enters and exits `async with ADAPTIVE_LLM_LIMIT:` in one coroutine. Pre-fix behavior (shared state) was strictly worse. A cross-task-safe design would need a token-returning API; out of scope for B1.\n2. **\"snapshot undercounts permits handed off to waiters\"** — **VERIFIED, fixed in this PR (docstring + test only, no behavior change).** Reproduced on CPython 3.11, 3.12 and 3.13: between `release()` handing a permit to a parked waiter and that waiter resuming, the permit is in neither `available` nor `held`. The original docstring over-claimed the two-term invariant as unconditional; follow-up commit `988de36` states it precisely and pins the window with a test. No permit is ever lost.\n3. Bot's \"health regression\" note on `_assert_can_fill()` (10 callers) is a test helper reused across the test module; no action.\n\n### Commits\n- `b711fc8` — the FZ-04 fix + tests (the originally verified B1 commit, unchanged)\n- `988de36` — docstring precision + hand-off-window test (from review item 2)\n- `c4d62b3` — merge of `main` (`69df55f`, docs-only `KNOWN_ISSUES.md`) required by branch protection (`strict`); history not rewritten\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01Q9gxgWEp4VAGfNHHQDfuz1\n\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code)_",
